## Framing note (not a summary)

Most of the Council will treat the thesis as an acoustics-and-PA-policy problem. That framing is correct and insufficient. A common quiet-by-design standard, if MWAA writes it well, is a **procurement instrument, a bond-rating narrative, an ADA-compliance moat, an airline-relations chip, and a federal-storytelling asset** in one document. The reason to do it now is not that quiet is trendy. The reason is that the next four years put nearly every major MWAA terminal decision — Concourse C/D replacement at IAD, the DCA Terminal 1 concourse rebuild, Concourse E fitout, and the AeroTrain extension — through design phases where a standard adopted early costs nothing and a standard argued as a change order costs a lot. This brief maps who else wins when MWAA gets that timing right.

---

## 1. The lattice — connections the thesis activates

**a. MWAA Office of Engineering (Design Manual, 2020, seven volumes) ↔ Quiet-by-design standard ↔ Airline Use & Lease cost recovery.** The Design Manual already has "force of law on the airport property"; a new volume or amendment is the cheapest binding vehicle MWAA has. It also converts quiet requirements into design-phase scope items — recoverable through the rate base under the 2025 U&L — instead of operational costs airlines will fight later. *Why it matters:* the vehicle is already built and the recovery mechanism is already agreed to.

**b. United Airlines (IAD anchor; 14 gates at Concourse E; $20B+ transformation partner) ↔ Common gate-podium and lounge audio spec ↔ Their own labor cost.** United gate agents make most of the announcements passengers complain about; they don't want to make them either. A standard that gives United one voice, one script library, one podium spec across IAD gates is a labor and consistency win for them. *Why it matters:* the loudest opposition to reduced-announcement policies at U.S. airports has historically been carriers — this reframes the same policy as a carrier operational improvement.

**c. TSA Cares / The Arc's Wings for Autism program ↔ MWAA quiet-zone and low-stimulation-space standard ↔ IAD/DCA passenger-experience differentiation.** Both programs already partner airports with disability communities. A quiet-by-design standard gives MWAA a tangible commitment — measurable spaces, not aspirational rooms — to anchor these partnerships at both airports. *Why it matters:* accessibility is where "quiet done wrong" becomes federal legal exposure; a standard done right becomes federal legal cover.

**d. Concessions program (dwell-time revenue) ↔ Muted-TV and sound-footprint rules ↔ Non-aeronautical revenue line.** Consultants will assume concessionaires oppose muted televisions. The Copenhagen and Helsinki evidence is that dwell and spend go up in calmer terminals, not down. If MWAA writes the standard with a concessions carve-out tier (clubs, restaurants with enclosed audio zones) it converts a natural opposition into a co-signer. *Why it matters:* the non-aero line is where MWAA compensates for the coverage compression Fitch and Moody's flagged (1.63x → ~1.3x over five years).

**e. Post-January 29, 2025 memorial context at DCA ↔ Passenger-confidence communication ↔ Redundant visual and staffed channels.** DCA passengers boarded aircraft for eighteen months under the shadow of PSA 5342. A standard that makes clear "we did not eliminate communication — we made it more intelligible, more accessible, and more staffed" is defensible in a way "quiet airport" branding is not. *Why it matters:* the political altitude of any DCA passenger-experience decision after the collision is different; the standard has to speak to that altitude in its opening pages, not defensively in an appendix.

**f. NTSB / FAA / DCA airspace changes (helicopter route removals, runway 15/33 and 4/22 restrictions) ↔ IROPS messaging load ↔ Announcement governance.** Post-collision airspace changes plus 10 new beyond-perimeter slot pairs mean DCA irregular operations are more frequent and more announcement-generating. A standard that specifies zoned override and script library architecture is the design response to a regulatory reality that isn't going away. *Why it matters:* a good standard is *counter-cyclical* — it costs least when adopted before the operations get harder, and DCA's operations are getting harder.

**g. ACRP Report 175 authoring community ↔ MWAA field-verification data ↔ National influence.** ACRP 175 is the standing U.S. reference on PA intelligibility; the next-generation revision is the natural place for MWAA to position itself. A commissioning-and-verification program at IAD and DCA becomes the primary dataset the sector doesn't yet have. *Why it matters:* MWAA can move from standard-taker to standard-shaper for less than the cost of one baggage-system change order.

**h. Historic-preservation constituency for the Saarinen Main Terminal (NRHP-listed 1978; AIA Honor Award 1966) ↔ Acoustic character preservation ↔ Docomomo / architectural community.** The Saarinen Main Terminal is a documented modernist icon whose interior character is partly acoustic (the great volume, the reflective planes, the calm). A standard that names the Main Terminal as a *reference space* rather than a problem to be retrofitted converts preservation groups from suspicious observers into allies of the standards program. *Why it matters:* the White House and DOT chose to feature Saarinen prominently in the July 29, 2026 IAD announcement; MWAA gains by giving preservation reviewers a doctrine that treats the building as a design lodestar.

