## 2. Three pattern matches — where this has happened before

### (i) Wallace Sabine and Boston Symphony Hall, 1900

Before 1900, acoustics was a *complaint*. A building was built, an audience found it unlistenable, and consultants were called to add drapes. Wallace Sabine — a Harvard physicist with no acoustical training — was pulled into fixing Harvard's Fogg lecture hall, discovered the reverberation-time formula (RT60), and applied it *before* construction to a new hall on Massachusetts Avenue. Boston Symphony Hall opened in 1900 and has been in continuous ranked contention for the best-sounding room in the world for 126 years, alongside Vienna's Musikverein and Amsterdam's Concertgebouw.

**The mapping.** MWAA's Design Manual is Sabine's formula: a way to convert what is otherwise argued as a subjective complaint ("this terminal is loud") into an objective specification ("STI ≥ 0.50 in departure lounges; reverberation time bounded; ambient LAeq bounded per zone") that binds at design phase, not at handover. The Sabine analogy is more than a rhetorical flourish — it names the historical *inflection*: acoustics stopped being a retrofit discipline and became a specification discipline the moment someone wrote the math down. The quiet-by-design standard is MWAA writing the math down, one century after Sabine did it for concert halls and forty years after ACRP began doing it for airports.

### (ii) HCAHPS "Quiet at Night" and hospital reimbursement

In 2006, CMS launched the HCAHPS patient-experience survey. One question — "During this hospital stay, how often was the area around your room quiet at night?" — turned out to be the lowest-scoring item nationwide. When Medicare tied 25% of the Value-Based Purchasing Total Performance Score to HCAHPS and put up to 2% of Medicare reimbursement at risk, hospitals responded. They redesigned nursing workflows, replaced overhead paging with vibrating pagers, added ceiling absorbers, and — critically — began *measuring* ambient sound. The measurement produced the redesign, not the other way around.

**The mapping.** Airports currently have no equivalent. There is no CMS in aviation, but there is Skytrax, there is ACI-ASQ, there are the airline satisfaction scores that airport authorities present to their boards, and there is the airport-directed bond rating narrative Fitch, S&P, and Moody's already read for enterprise risk. If MWAA writes a standard with **numerical quiet-KPIs reported quarterly to the board**, it accomplishes what HCAHPS accomplished in medicine: it converts a soft topic into a measured topic, and measured topics get resources. The mapping is precise — mandatory survey question, tied to public score, tied to money. MWAA can build a lighter version of the same architecture without waiting for a federal mandate.

### (iii) Wireless Emergency Alerts (WEA) and geo-targeted messaging

In 2006 the WARN Act created what is now the WEA system, run jointly by FEMA and the FCC. Its central design idea is *zoned override authority*. Under WEA 3.0 (2019+), an alert originator can specify a target polygon and wireless providers must deliver alerts to that shape with less than one-tenth of a mile of overshoot. Ordinary broadcast messaging goes to the cell segment; emergency messaging cuts through it. The zoning is a legal and technical *architecture*, not a discretion.

**The mapping.** The single hardest question inside a quiet-terminal standard is: *how do we preserve the ability to issue a genuine emergency communication that penetrates every space, while eliminating the announcement spillover that produces the noise complaint?* WEA answers this by separating **origination authority** (who can call the shape), **delivery zoning** (which speakers or devices receive it), and **override behavior** (what other content it interrupts). MWAA's quiet-by-design standard should adopt WEA's *architectural* pattern even though the technology stack is different: PA zoning as a first-class design object, an override register that names who can page which zone, and a distinction between operational messages, gate messages, wayfinding messages, and life-safety messages that lives in the design spec, not in an operator's habit. This is how "we made it quieter" and "we did not make it less safe" become the same sentence rather than opposing ones.

