## 4. Collaboration paths — from opposition to alignment

### Opposition 1 — Airlines (led by United at IAD, American at DCA), worried about missed boardings and airline-recovery cost

*What they actually need:* reduced gate-agent voice fatigue, fewer inconsistent announcements across gates, less passenger-service labor spent repeating information, and no exposure on missed-boarding metrics.

*What MWAA offers:* a common gate-podium spec with pre-approved script library; SFO benchmark data showing missed-boarding rates did not deteriorate under its 40% announcement reduction; treatment of gate-final-call announcements as a *reserved* zone under the standard, not a target for elimination; explicit airline-consultation clause in the standards governance document.

*Convener and first meeting:* Chief Operating Officer, MWAA. First meeting is a joint session with United and American station managers and IAD/DCA operations chiefs — not procurement or public affairs. Agenda is technical: which announcements can go, which must stay, which zone does what. The philosophical debate happens once the operations conversation is finished.

### Opposition 2 — Public-safety and emergency-management community, worried that a quiet-airport policy weakens emergency communications

*What they actually need:* absolute clarity on override authority, verified STI in life-safety zones, redundant channels (visual, mobile, staffed), documentation that will survive an NTSB or FAA post-incident review — especially post-January 29.

*What MWAA offers:* the WEA-derived architectural pattern (zoned origination + override register + life-safety carve-outs); explicit STI thresholds in life-safety zones set higher than the general-terminal minimum; commissioning verification with the airport fire marshal and public-safety director as sign-off authorities; a written incorporation into the airport emergency plan (AEP), not a parallel document.

*Convener and first meeting:* Director of Public Safety. First meeting is a table-top exercise using a real incident scenario against the draft zoning map. The output is not agreement — it is a red-lined map, which becomes the standard's first evidence base.

### Opposition 3 — Concessionaires and airline clubs, worried muted TVs and sound-footprint rules will reduce dwell revenue

*What they actually need:* evidence dwell doesn't drop, protection of enclosed audio zones inside clubs and enclosed dining, predictable enforcement, and a voice in commissioning.

*What MWAA offers:* the Nordic and Copenhagen dwell-and-spend evidence base as opening data; a tiered standard that carves out enclosed audio zones and makes the rule about *spillover*, not about *silence*; concessions-industry seat on the Design Manual amendment working group; a phased in-lease compliance schedule tied to normal lease renewal cycles, not a compliance cliff.

*Convener and first meeting:* Chief Commercial Officer, MWAA. First meeting is with the concessions master partners at IAD and the DCA concessions program manager — not with individual operators. The negotiation happens once at the program level and cascades through sublease renewals.

